def tinmanx_white_paper_markdown(prompt):
    return "\n".join(
        [
            "# TinmanX Product Consolidation White Paper",
            "",
            "## Request",
            "",
            str(prompt or "").strip(),
            "",
            "## Executive Direction",
            "",
            "TinmanX should become the single user-facing product name while preserving the Orca/TinmanX functionality already built. The goal is not to erase technical lineage; it is to make the product feel coherent, professional, and owned under one roof.",
            "",
            "## Current State To Preserve",
            "",
            "- Existing slicer functionality and printer/profile behavior.",
            "- TinmanX profile work, material presets, calibration workflows, and printer-specific knowledge.",
            "- Orca-derived compatibility where it keeps existing projects, profiles, and settings usable.",
            "- Local Codex CLI UI tooling, package-health checks, generated-output links, and regression tests.",
            "",
            "## Target Architecture",
            "",
            "- User-facing product: TinmanX.",
            "- App naming: TinmanX in the title, dock/app label, dialogs, visible settings, generated reports, and release package.",
            "- Profile namespace: TinmanX-owned printer, filament, process, and material presets while preserving migration aliases for older Orca/TinmanX paths.",
            "- Documentation: TinmanX docs first, with an attribution section for upstream Orca/Open-source components.",
            "- Release package: one installer/archive with app, local server, tool inventory, source-vault references, tests, and startup scripts.",
            "",
            "## Rename Map",
            "",
            "| Area | Old/legacy wording | TinmanX wording | Notes |",
            "| --- | --- | --- | --- |",
            "| App title | Orca / Orca Codex / OrcaSlicer Codex | TinmanX | Keep compatibility strings only where required by file formats. |",
            "| User docs | Orca/TinmanX | TinmanX | Put lineage and credit in attribution, not primary workflow text. |",
            "| Profile folders | OrcaSlicer-Codex, Orca legacy paths | TinmanX profile store | Provide migration/import path before deleting old names. |",
            "| Reports | Generic prompt slug or Orca wording | Professional TinmanX task title | Use human titles such as Filament Profile Tuning or Backup Power Diagram. |",
            "| Releases | Mixed naming | TinmanX release bundle | Include version, scope, credits, and install notes. |",
            "",
            "## Functional Requirements",
            "",
            "- Keep all existing slicer workflows usable after the rename.",
            "- Keep printer, filament, process, and machine presets visible inside the installed app.",
            "- Keep generated files clickable in Codex CLI UI.",
            "- Keep local-only/free tooling boundaries intact.",
            "- Keep regression tests for 3D printing, CAD, CFD, power systems, Mac/device repair, and TinmanX itself.",
            "- Keep source-vault manuals and stable references local so known answers are not repeatedly researched.",
            "",
            "## Legal And Attribution Boundary",
            "",
            "TinmanX branding can be primary in the user experience, but upstream credit must remain in About, license files, source headers, release notes, and documentation. Removing user-facing Orca wording does not mean removing required copyright, license, or attribution notices.",
            "",
            "## Migration Plan",
            "",
            "1. Inventory every visible Orca/OrcaSlicer/Orca Codex string in app UI, docs, scripts, reports, profile metadata, release files, and generated templates.",
            "2. Classify each string as user-facing brand, compatibility/internal, legal attribution, or file-format requirement.",
            "3. Rename user-facing brand strings to TinmanX.",
            "4. Preserve legal attribution and compatibility strings where required.",
            "5. Add profile migration so older Orca/TinmanX profile stores can be imported or read without data loss.",
            "6. Rebuild the app bundle and verify installed-app visibility, not just source files.",
            "7. Run package health, golden tests, profile visibility checks, and one real slice/import smoke test.",
            "",
            "## Test Plan",
            "",
            "- App launches as TinmanX.",
            "- Dock/app/icon/title use TinmanX branding.",
            "- Existing printer, filament, and process presets are still visible.",
            "- A migrated project opens without losing printer/material/process settings.",
            "- Search for user-facing Orca strings returns only approved attribution or compatibility locations.",
            "- Package health passes.",
            "- Golden tests for TinmanX routing, profile tuning, file attachments, generated outputs, and Codex-style answer etiquette pass.",
            "",
            "## Rollback Plan",
            "",
            "- Keep a backup of the current app bundle and profile store before migration.",
            "- Keep a rename manifest so every changed path/string can be reversed.",
            "- Keep compatibility aliases until at least one verified release proves user data migrates cleanly.",
            "",
            "## Done Means",
            "",
            "This work is done when TinmanX is the visible product, all existing workflows still work, attribution is preserved, profiles migrate cleanly, the app package passes health checks, and a real installed-app smoke test confirms the user experience is coherent.",
            "",
        ]
    )


def stage_tinmanx_white_paper(messages):
    prompt = latest_user_text(messages)
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%d-%H%M%S")
    slug = slugify("tinmanx product consolidation white paper", "tinmanx-white-paper")
    target = LOCAL_PRODUCT_DOC_OUTPUT_DIR / f"{stamp}-{slug}"
    target.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    white_paper_path = target / "TinmanX_Product_Consolidation_White_Paper.md"
    white_paper_path.write_text(tinmanx_white_paper_markdown(prompt), encoding="utf-8")
    return white_paper_path


