def minimum_wind_four_rotors_direct_answer(messages):
    if not is_minimum_wind_four_rotors_question(messages):
        return ""
    return "\n\n".join(
        [
            "Minimum wind speed cannot be proven from `four rotors` alone. A practical starting estimate is about 3-5 mph for visible startup on a well-balanced low-wind VAWT, but useful loaded generation may need more.",
            (
                "This is why: adding four rotors mostly increases swept area and available torque. It does not automatically lower cut-in speed unless bearing friction, generator cogging, blade drag/lift, rotor inertia, and load torque are also controlled. Four rotors can help the generator stay loaded without stalling, but the exact minimum wind still needs measured RPM and shaft torque."
            ),
            (
                "Use this as the engineering gate: test unloaded startup at 2, 3, 4, and 5 mph; then repeat with the generator connected and measure RPM, shaft torque, rectified voltage, and load current. Do not claim CFD-verified cut-in unless the solver or wind test has actually been run for this geometry."
            ),
            (
                "You should also consider: if the goal is 60 VDC at very low wind, the generator winding, gearing, rectifier losses, and boost converter may matter more than adding rotor count."
            ),
        ]
    )


