def wind_torque_rpm_followup_direct_answer(messages):
    if not is_wind_torque_rpm_followup(messages):
        return ""
    query = latest_user_text(messages).lower()
    if text_has_any(query, ("3 mph", "3mph", "7 mph", "7mph", "15 mph", "15mph")):
        return "\n\n".join(
            [
                "Using the current about-518 mm rotor diameter assumption, a low-speed VAWT at TSR 0.8-1.2 would be roughly 40-59 rpm at 3 mph, 92-139 rpm at 7 mph, and 198-297 rpm at 15 mph.",
                (
                    "This is why: RPM comes from `RPM = TSR x wind_speed / radius x 60 / (2*pi)`. With a 0.259 m radius, the speed factor is about 16.5 rpm per mph at TSR 1.0. A drag-heavy or starting-focused rotor may run lower; a cleaner lift-style rotor can run higher."
                ),
                (
                    "You should also consider: adding two rotors below the existing rotor should help torque and loaded power because it increases swept area, but it will not automatically raise free RPM. It may even lower unloaded RPM slightly from extra drag and inertia; the win is that the generator can stay loaded without stalling the rotor as easily."
                ),
            ]
        )
    return "\n\n".join(
        [
            "Yes. The next pass should optimize the rotor and generator together around shaft power, not just maximum RPM or maximum torque by itself.",
            (
                "This is why: electrical output comes from mechanical power, and mechanical power is torque times angular speed. "
                "A rotor that makes high RPM with no torque will stall under load, while a high-torque/low-RPM rotor may never reach the generator voltage target."
            ),
            (
                "You should also consider: pick the target wind speed, estimate rotor TSR/RPM, match generator Kv or gearing to that RPM, then validate with measured RPM, torque, rectifier losses, and load voltage before changing blade geometry."
            ),
        ]
    )


